# approach.rules — yamlmuster ruleset for SPAR approach YAML: the approach
# vocabulary and structural checks, loaded by spar::_yamlmuster_approach and
# driven by spar::validate_approach. It declares its own `level root` and
# loads into a dedicated yamlmuster instance; two rulesets that each declare
# `root` cannot share one instance, which is why every document kind gets its
# own singleton.
#
# ── PROJECTION CONSTRAINT (State render-path invariant) ─────────────────
# Render-path callers validate spar::State's approach_summary projection
# (project_approach_data, spar-state.tcl:502-545), not a fresh parse.
# That projection PRESERVES the whole key skeleton at every level but
# BLANKS these VALUES to keep the cache small:
#     • email message  body        (linkedin bodies are kept)
#     • message         reply_summary
#     • script_item     text
#     • reply           text
# So no rule below may read those blanked values. Every value a rule reads
# (message `to`, `mode`, `actioned_date`, round `type`,
# message `channel`, linkedin `text`/`body`, `parent.message_id`, root
# `profile_hash`) is a preserved field; the existence-only checks
# (email_missing_content, vocab) read kept keys. Verified field-by-field
# against the projection.
#
# Selection: every rule carries -groups {approach}; the singleton is driven
# with `validate <adata> -groups {approach} ...`.
# Emission order: yamlmuster fires every rule at a node before descending into
# its children, so root aggregates (missing_decisions, no_final_round,
# placeholder_to, profile_hash_*) emit ahead of the deeper decisions / round /
# message issues, and declaration order fixes the sequence within a level. The
# gate (-severities error -limit 1) therefore surfaces a root-level error
# ahead of any deeper one on a document that carries both.

# ── Levels (closed vocabulary) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Declared in the SAME order as _approach_canonical_keys so the built-in
# wrong_level hint resolves a misplaced key to the FIRST-declared owning
# level, matching legacy's dict-order `break` (spar-validate.tcl:74-77).
level root                 -keys {decisions rounds angle_rationale response_likelihood a_note r_note fact_provenance quality_checklist profile_hash}
level decisions            -keys {channel language angle sender channel_detail subsegment}
level round                -keys {type number messages verdict fact_check in_character chosen_usps revision_note notes replies antifact_check}
level message              -keys {channel subject body to actioned_date replied_date reply_summary script text bcc cc director_note to_note phone_note mode parent reply_all}
level parent               -keys {account folder uid message_id references subject from to cc}
level fact_provenance_item -keys {claim source}
level fact_check_item      -keys {claim source result note correction}
level script_item          -keys {point text}

# ── Nesting edges (drive traversal + wrong_level hints) ─────────────────
# Child order at each level = legacy descent order, so vocab issues emit in
# the legacy sequence. NOTE: round.replies is deliberately NOT an edge.
# Legacy never vocab-walks replies (it is a valid round key, no descent).
child root    decisions       dict decisions
child root    rounds          list round
child root    fact_provenance list fact_provenance_item
child round   messages        list message
child round   fact_check      list fact_check_item
child message script          list script_item
child message parent          dict parent

# ── ROOT level rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# vocab walk at root. Built-in codes unknown_key / wrong_level. The host
# wrapper rewrites unknown_key into unknown_key_<level> using the issue's
# `level` field. The built-in messages MUST be the legacy strings:
#   unknown_key : "unknown key '<k>' at <level> — not in canonical vocabulary"
#   wrong_level : "'<k>' at <level> belongs at <found_at>; move it there"
rule vocab root -groups {approach}

# missing_decisions: legacy 187-190. Existence only.
rule require root decisions -code missing_decisions \
    -message {Approach file missing required 'decisions' key} -groups {approach}

# missing_rounds (two messages) + no_final_round: legacy 193-216. A host
# predicate, not the declarative require/-nonempty + any pair: legacy emits
# two distinct messages ("missing required 'rounds' key" when absent, "has
# empty 'rounds' array" when empty) and RETURNS after either, suppressing
# no_final_round. A require rule carries one message and cannot early-return,
# and an `any` rule fires no_final_round on an empty (but present) rounds
# list. The predicate reproduces all three cases exactly, including the
# suppression of no_final_round on absent/empty rounds. Declared after
# missing_decisions so the gate's first error on a doc missing both keys is
# missing_decisions, matching legacy.
rule predicate root rounds_structural -code no_final_round \
    -message {Approach file has no round with type: final} -groups {approach}

# placeholder_to (two variants) plus email_desync: legacy 320-363. HOST
# PREDICATE. Escapes the DSL because: (1) it reads `to_addresses` as
# gathered by analyse_final_round (FINAL rounds, channel email, non-null
# `to` only), a cross-level aggregation a message-level regexp rule cannot
# scope; (2) the three per-address checks are mutually exclusive via
# `continue`, so independent rules would double-emit; (3) email_desync's
# message interpolates the roster_email CONTEXT value, not a node field.
# Splitting this into three rules (regexp for the shape test, predicate for
# the stub-lists, predicate for desync) is therefore not viable: one root
# predicate reproduces legacy exactly. No -needs: placeholder checks run
# regardless of roster_email; the predicate reads roster_email from
# -context and skips the desync sub-check when it is empty or lacks '@'
# (legacy 357). The predicate emits, overriding code/severity/message:
#   placeholder_to (error): "Approach file has non-email to: address '<a>'"
#   placeholder_to (error): "Approach to: '<a>' looks like a placeholder (reserved domain or stub local-part)"
#   email_desync   (warn) : "Approach to: '<a>' differs from roster email '<roster_email>'"
rule predicate root placeholder_to -code placeholder_to \
    -message {Approach file has non-email to: address} -groups {approach}

# profile_hash_misplaced: legacy 392-395. HOST PREDICATE
# (first_line_is_profile_hash) wrapping _approach_first_line_is_profile_hash
# (416-423): opens the file, tests line 1. -needs approach_path drops the
# rule for pure-dict callers (approach_path absent from -context). The
# predicate additionally guards `dict exists $node profile_hash` (legacy
# 384) and a non-empty path. Static message.
rule predicate root first_line_is_profile_hash -needs approach_path \
    -code profile_hash_misplaced \
    -message {profile_hash must be the first line of the approach file (issue #63) — re-emit with the hash on line 1} \
    -groups {approach}

# profile_hash_mismatch: legacy 396-405. HOST PREDICATE (profile_hash_actual)
# wrapping the sha256 sibling-profile compare. The message interpolates two
# hashes (stored, actual), not template-expressible, so it is a predicate.
# -needs approach_path; guards profile_hash presence and sibling-profile
# existence internally. Predicate builds:
#   "Approach profile_hash 'sha256:<stored>' does not match profile file (current sha256:<actual>) — re-approach required"
rule predicate root profile_hash_actual -needs approach_path \
    -code profile_hash_mismatch \
    -message {Approach profile_hash does not match profile file (re-approach required)} \
    -groups {approach}

# ── DECISIONS level ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab decisions -groups {approach}

# ── ROUND level rules ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab round -groups {approach}

# draft_missing_number / review_missing_number: legacy 226-233. Warnings.
# -when gates on the round's own `type` (sibling field).
rule require round number -when {type draft} -severity warning \
    -code draft_missing_number \
    -message {Draft round missing required 'number' field} -groups {approach}
rule require round number -when {type review} -severity warning \
    -code review_missing_number \
    -message {Review round missing required 'number' field} -groups {approach}

# linkedin_note_too_long: legacy 285-308. HOST
# PREDICATE at ROUND level: it needs the round `type`
# (final) AND iterates the round's messages, filtering channel linkedin and
# actioned-null; the text/body fallback and mode gate are "fallback-
# tangled", so a plain `length` rule cannot express it. -when {type final}
# gates the round; the predicate does the per-message linkedin work and
# emits, overriding code/message per offending message:
#   linkedin_note_too_long: "LinkedIn invite note is <n> chars, limit 300; shorten it, or declare mode: dm if a direct message to a 1st-degree connection is intended"
# ORDERING: as a round-level rule it fires before descent into the message
# level, so its errors precede email_missing_content and
# reply_missing_parent_message_id.
rule predicate round linkedin_guard -when {type final} \
    -code linkedin_note_too_long \
    -message {LinkedIn invite note exceeds the 300-char invite limit} -groups {approach}

# too_many_final_emails: legacy 314-317. At most one channel==email message
# in a final round. %n = the measured count.
# ORDERING: round-level rule fires before message descent.
rule atmost round messages 1 -where {channel email} -when {type final} \
    -code too_many_final_emails \
    -message {Final round has %n email messages; maximum is 1} -groups {approach}

# ── MESSAGE level rules ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab message -groups {approach}

# email_missing_content (reply variant): legacy 248-251. Existence only
# (body value is blanked in the projection, but the KEY is preserved).
rule require message body -when {channel email mode reply} -severity warning \
    -code email_missing_content \
    -message {Reply email message missing 'body'} -groups {approach}

# reply_missing_parent_message_id: legacy 252-257. Nested keypath walked
# with -nonblank: absent parent, absent message_id, or blank message_id all
# fire (legacy's getdef chain plus `string trim eq ""`). parent.message_id
# is preserved in the projection.
rule require message {parent message_id} -nonblank -when {channel email mode reply} \
    -code reply_missing_parent_message_id \
    -message {Reply email message has no parent.message_id; cannot thread the reply} \
    -groups {approach}

# email_missing_content (non-reply variant): legacy 259-262. -anyof means
# the {subject body} list is ALTERNATIVES (fires only when NEITHER key
# exists); existence only. -unless {mode reply} excludes the reply branch.
rule require message {subject body} -anyof -when {channel email} -unless {mode reply} \
    -severity warning -code email_missing_content \
    -message {Email message missing both 'subject' and 'body'} -groups {approach}

# ── PARENT level ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab parent -groups {approach}

# ── FACT_CHECK_ITEM level ───────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab fact_check_item -groups {approach}

# ── FACT_PROVENANCE_ITEM level ──────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab fact_provenance_item -groups {approach}

# ── SCRIPT_ITEM level ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
rule vocab script_item -groups {approach}
